<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Mod id="metropolis-ascendant" version="4"
	xmlns="ModInfo">
	<Properties>
		<Name>Metropolis Ascendant</Name>
		<Description>Metropolis Ascendant — one metropolis, every victory. Build few cities and pour everything into making them extraordinary.
[N][N]Most of Civ VII rewards going wide. Metropolis Ascendant flips that: you build few cities and pour everything into making them extraordinary — a dense, ever-growing metropolis that out-thinks, out-builds and out-cultures empires many times its size. It began as a tall overhaul layered onto the game's own trees; it has grown into a tall civilization system of its own. Your bonuses run at full strength only while your footprint stays within your earned allowance, and switch off the moment you overreach.
[N][N]KEY MECHANICS
[N]- The Ascendancy Tree — your metropolis researches its own civic tree: three roots, deepening masteries and a repeatable capstone, re-keyed to every Age. Route toward Science, Culture, Economy, Expansion, Military or Diplomacy and the city specializes accordingly. Every node's panel states exactly what it grants.
[N]- Civic Boosts — every civic on the tree carries a Boost, a goal shown right on the node in the spirit of Civ VI's boosts. Steer your play toward it and that civic researches faster.
[N]- Feats, Charters &amp; Dedications — fifteen Major feats mark the achievements of a towering city, and their rewards land the moment the Triumph fires: a Tradition with an extra slot, a named bonus package, a settlement Charter, or a next-Age Dedication. Charters are how you expand: you begin with a single city and earn your way toward more, never past four. Never map luck; always something you earned.
[N]- Traditions — custom government cards written for one dense city, from steady per-population yields to sharp tradeoffs; the feat-earned ones carry across the Age transition and stack.
[N]- Custom Pantheons — Goddess of the Plantation (+1 Culture on Plantations) and Mistress of the Beasts (+1 Happiness and +1 Production on Pastures and Camps), each with its own icon in the base chooser.
[N]- Urban Population is power — your city's Urban Population (not rural) drives everything; specialists pay out full tile adjacency, so a dense city compounds fast.
[N]- Suzerain layer — bonuses scale with how many city-states you lead: reach, resources and influence without founding another city.
[N]- Happiness pays off — keep the metropolis Joyous and it earns escalating Science and Culture per Urban Population (more while Ecstatic); specialists also cost less to maintain until the city is thriving.
[N]- Wonders &amp; Arcadia — concentrate Wonders and natural beauty in one city, a strength a sprawling AI empire can't match. Wonders make adjacent Buildings happier and beautify the land; discover a Natural Wonder and Arcadia opens up — Breathtaking rural tiles, Buildings beside Mountains, and even terraced Mountain peaks all become productive (scaling each Age). Arcadia also embraces the waters — rivers, lakes, the open sea and reefs each yield by type, you can work the open ocean in Exploration, and coastal cities gain a flat yield floor, so a water-heavy or island map is never a dead end.
[N]- Reach beyond your borders — build a Surveyor to claim a resource tile up to 5 tiles from your Settlements into your metropolis (spent after one claim), so one dense city can command resources a wide empire would spread across many.
[N]- Raze what you can't hold — a dense city can't keep conquests, so razing becomes a real tool: take a city by force for a one-time burst of Gold and Influence, pillage its buildings for more, and raze it in a turn or two. Tied to the tall playstyle — a conquer-and-keep empire gains none of it.
[N]- Every victory stays open — if a packed metropolis has no open tile for a Modern victory Wonder, raise its Foundations over an existing district to complete it in place (only once you've earned the Wonder normally, never a shortcut), so all four victory paths stay reachable when you go tall.
[N]- Know where you stand — a built-in Bonus Dashboard (button in the sub-system dock) shows every bonus for the current Age: what's active and what's next, your Ascendancy and mastery progress, the Settlements-vs-allowance track, boosted civics, tier progress and happiness at a glance. Install leonardfactory's Policy Yields Preview mod alongside and every bonus also shows a computed per-turn figure.
[N]- The allowance rule — every bonus pays across all your settlements while you are at or under your earned allowance (Antiquity starts at 1; the floor is 2 from Exploration; feats earn the rest, hard max 4), and everything switches off the instant you exceed it.
[N]- Spans all three Ages: Antiquity, Exploration and Modern.
[N][N]Every Ascendancy civic states its bonuses and its Boost on the node panel, and every Triumph states exactly what it grants — you always know what you are building toward.
[N][N]GROWTH MILESTONE — at 5 Urban Population in Antiquity, 8 in Exploration, 10 in Modern: your metropolis gains +10/+12/+15 Happiness (by Age), and its Specialists cost 50% less Food and Happiness upkeep until the city reaches Ecstatic.
[N][N]Built and tested on Civilization VII 1.4.2. No DLC required. Experimental and actively tuned — feedback welcome.
[N][N]Built and developed using Claude Code.</Description>
		<Authors>Chris</Authors>
		<Package>Mod</Package>
		<AffectsSavedGames>1</AffectsSavedGames>
		<Version>1</Version>
		<LastUpdated>2026-07-04</LastUpdated>
	</Properties>

	<Dependencies>
		<Mod id="base-standard" title="LOC_MODULE_BASE_STANDARD_NAME"/>
	</Dependencies>
	<References>
		<Mod id="core" title="LOC_MODULE_CORE_NAME"/>
	</References>

	<ActionCriteria>
		<Criteria id="age-antiquity">
			<AgeInUse>AGE_ANTIQUITY</AgeInUse>
		</Criteria>
		<Criteria id="age-exploration">
			<AgeInUse>AGE_EXPLORATION</AgeInUse>
		</Criteria>
		<Criteria id="age-modern">
			<AgeInUse>AGE_MODERN</AgeInUse>
		</Criteria>
		<Criteria id="always">
			<AlwaysMet/>
		</Criteria>
	</ActionCriteria>

	<ActionGroups>
		<ActionGroup id="text-all" scope="game" criteria="always">
			<Properties>
				<LoadOrder>100</LoadOrder>
			</Properties>
			<Actions>
				<UpdateText>
					<Item>text/en_us/MetropolisAscendantText.xml</Item>
					<Item>text/en_us/MetropolisAscendantNotes.generated.xml</Item>
					<Item>text/en_us/AscendancyText.generated.xml</Item>
					<Item>text/en_us/PantheonText.xml</Item>
				</UpdateText>
			</Actions>
		</ActionGroup>

		<!-- Icons for the victory-wonder "Foundations" buildings (issue #1). Icons load via UpdateIcons (the icon DB),
		     globally (criteria="always") like the base game's art groups, not per-age. Reuses each target Wonder's blp icon. -->
		<ActionGroup id="recycle-icons" scope="game" criteria="always">
			<Actions>
				<!-- Surveyor custom glyph (2026-07-12): raw PNG + extensionless twin, imported in BOTH scopes
				     (Ireland-mod convention - the engine references textures by bare ID in some spots). -->
				<ImportFiles>
					<Item>ui/icons/surveyor-flag.png</Item>
					<Item>ui/icons/surveyor-flag</Item>
					<Item>ui/icons/surveyor-portrait.png</Item>
					<Item>ui/icons/surveyor-portrait</Item>
				</ImportFiles>
				<UpdateIcons>
					<Item>data/icons/recycle-icons.xml</Item>
					<Item>data/icons/surveyor-icons.xml</Item>
				</UpdateIcons>
				<!-- Surveyor 3D model (issue #12): VisualRemaps register ONLY from criteria="always" groups (per-age
				     groups gave a black portrait/empty model), so the remap is global here, not per-age. Renders the
				     Surveyor as UNIT_SCOUT (base-standard, present every Age). Loads via its own UpdateVisualRemaps action. -->
				<UpdateVisualRemaps>
					<Item>data/surveyor-visualremap.xml</Item>
				</UpdateVisualRemaps>
			</Actions>
		</ActionGroup>

		<!-- SHELL-scope twin of the surveyor icon + visual remap (issue #12). REQUIRED: the unit-flag manager resolves
		     the unit's icon NAME via UI.getIconURL(..., "UNIT_FLAG") from the SHELL-scope icon database. Registering the
		     icon only in game scope (above) left the flag/portrait BLACK - the game never even requested blp:unitflag_scout
		     (proven in UI.log). The base game loads unit-icons.xml + visual-remaps.xml in BOTH game AND shell scope for
		     exactly this reason (base-game-main + base-game-shell). Same files, shell scope. -->
		<ActionGroup id="surveyor-shell" scope="shell" criteria="always">
			<Actions>
				<ImportFiles>
					<Item>ui/icons/surveyor-flag.png</Item>
					<Item>ui/icons/surveyor-flag</Item>
					<Item>ui/icons/surveyor-portrait.png</Item>
					<Item>ui/icons/surveyor-portrait</Item>
				</ImportFiles>
				<UpdateIcons>
					<Item>data/icons/recycle-icons.xml</Item>
					<Item>data/icons/surveyor-icons.xml</Item>
				</UpdateIcons>
				<UpdateVisualRemaps>
					<Item>data/surveyor-visualremap.xml</Item>
				</UpdateVisualRemaps>
			</Actions>
		</ActionGroup>

		<!-- Custom pantheon "Goddess of the Plantation" (2026-07-15). Icon (custom PNG) in an always
		     game-scope group like the other icons; belief + effect in an age-antiquity group (pantheons
		     are an Antiquity-only system). Belief auto-lists in the data-driven pantheon chooser. -->
		<ActionGroup id="pantheon-icons" scope="game" criteria="always">
			<Actions>
				<ImportFiles>
					<Item>ui/icons/pantheon-plantation.png</Item>
					<Item>ui/icons/pantheon-beasts.png</Item>
				</ImportFiles>
				<UpdateIcons>
					<Item>data/icons/pantheon-icons.xml</Item>
				</UpdateIcons>
			</Actions>
		</ActionGroup>

		<ActionGroup id="pantheon-aq" scope="game" criteria="age-antiquity">
			<Properties>
				<LoadOrder>108</LoadOrder>
			</Properties>
			<Actions>
				<UpdateDatabase>
					<Item>data/pantheon-gameeffects.xml</Item>
					<Item>data/pantheon.xml</Item>
				</UpdateDatabase>
			</Actions>
		</ActionGroup>

		<!-- Shared constructible-adjacency definitions in their OWN action group per age (LoadOrder 105, before the
		     modifiers at 110). ISOLATION: a data error here rolls back only this group, NOT the modifiers group, so a
		     bad row can't take the whole mod down (it did 2026-06-26: a wrong table name nuked the age group + crashed).
		     Kept per-age (not criteria="always") because the gameplay DB rebuilds each Age, so the rule must re-apply
		     per Age the same way the modifiers do. -->
		<ActionGroup id="antiquity-shared" scope="game" criteria="age-antiquity">
			<Properties>
				<LoadOrder>105</LoadOrder>
			</Properties>
			<Actions>
				<UpdateDatabase>
					<Item>data/shared/constructibles.xml</Item>
					<Item>data/antiquity/surveyor.xml</Item>
				</UpdateDatabase>
			</Actions>
		</ActionGroup>

		<!-- GEN-2 Major reward Traditions: ALWAYS scope so an earned card's definition exists in
		     every Age (G3 persistence law). LoadOrder 107 = after shared tables (105), before the
		     per-age files (110) that bind TraditionModifiers to these rows. -->
		<ActionGroup id="ascendancy-majors" scope="game" criteria="always">
			<Properties>
				<LoadOrder>107</LoadOrder>
			</Properties>
			<Actions>
				<UpdateDatabase>
					<Item>data/ascendancy-majors.xml</Item>
				</UpdateDatabase>
			</Actions>
		</ActionGroup>

		<ActionGroup id="antiquity" scope="game" criteria="age-antiquity">
			<Properties>
				<LoadOrder>110</LoadOrder>
			</Properties>
			<Actions>
				<UpdateDatabase>
					<Item>data/antiquity/modifiers.xml</Item>
					<Item>data/antiquity/traditions.xml</Item>
					<Item>data/antiquity/surveyor-bind.xml</Item>
					<Item>data/antiquity/ascendancy-effects.xml</Item>
					<Item>data/antiquity/ascendancy-tree.xml</Item>
					<Item>data/antiquity/ascendancy-reqweights.xml</Item>
				</UpdateDatabase>
			</Actions>
		</ActionGroup>

		<ActionGroup id="exploration-shared" scope="game" criteria="age-exploration">
			<Properties>
				<LoadOrder>105</LoadOrder>
			</Properties>
			<Actions>
				<UpdateDatabase>
					<Item>data/shared/constructibles.xml</Item>
					<Item>data/exploration/surveyor.xml</Item>
				</UpdateDatabase>
			</Actions>
		</ActionGroup>

		<ActionGroup id="exploration" scope="game" criteria="age-exploration">
			<Properties>
				<LoadOrder>110</LoadOrder>
			</Properties>
			<Actions>
				<UpdateDatabase>
					<Item>data/exploration/modifiers.xml</Item>
					<Item>data/exploration/traditions.xml</Item>
					<Item>data/exploration/surveyor-bind.xml</Item>
					<Item>data/exploration/ascendancy-effects.xml</Item>
					<Item>data/exploration/ascendancy-tree.xml</Item>
					<Item>data/exploration/ascendancy-reqweights.xml</Item>
				</UpdateDatabase>
			</Actions>
		</ActionGroup>

		<ActionGroup id="modern-shared" scope="game" criteria="age-modern">
			<Properties>
				<LoadOrder>105</LoadOrder>
			</Properties>
			<Actions>
				<UpdateDatabase>
					<Item>data/shared/constructibles.xml</Item>
					<Item>data/modern/surveyor.xml</Item>
				</UpdateDatabase>
			</Actions>
		</ActionGroup>

		<ActionGroup id="modern" scope="game" criteria="age-modern">
			<Properties>
				<LoadOrder>110</LoadOrder>
			</Properties>
			<Actions>
				<UpdateDatabase>
					<Item>data/modern/modifiers.xml</Item>
					<Item>data/modern/traditions.xml</Item>
					<Item>data/modern/recycle.xml</Item>
					<Item>data/modern/surveyor-bind.xml</Item>
					<Item>data/modern/ascendancy-effects.xml</Item>
					<Item>data/modern/ascendancy-tree.xml</Item>
					<Item>data/modern/ascendancy-reqweights.xml</Item>
				</UpdateDatabase>
			</Actions>
		</ActionGroup>

		<!-- BONUS DASHBOARD (folded in from the ma-bonus-dashboard standalone 2026-07-04; hand-edited groups -
		     gen-ascendant's modinfo sync only patches version/thresholds). Shell scope carries the Options→Mods
		     entries, the F7 hotkey rows and the LOC strings the options screen needs; no per-age criteria because
		     the panel detects the current Age at runtime. -->
		<ActionGroup id="dashboard-shell" scope="shell" criteria="always">
			<Properties>
				<LoadOrder>2000</LoadOrder>
			</Properties>
			<Actions>
				<UIScripts>
					<Item>ui/options/mad-options.js</Item>
				</UIScripts>
				<UpdateDatabase>
					<Item>config/input.xml</Item>
				</UpdateDatabase>
				<UpdateText>
					<Item>text/en_us/DashboardText.xml</Item>
				</UpdateText>
			</Actions>
		</ActionGroup>
		<ActionGroup id="dashboard-game" scope="game" criteria="always">
			<Properties>
				<LoadOrder>2000</LoadOrder>
			</Properties>
			<Actions>
				<ImportFiles>
					<Item>ui/dashboard/mad-dashboard.html</Item>
					<Item>ui/dashboard/mad-dashboard.css</Item>
					<Item>ui/dock/mad-dock.css</Item>
					<Item>ui/icons/mad-dock-icon.png</Item>
					<Item>ui/cards/mad-card-brand.css</Item>
				</ImportFiles>
				<UIScripts>
					<Item>ui/options/mad-options.js</Item>
					<!-- Generated yield-preview id manifest (gen-ascendancy.ps1); read lazily by the dashboard
					     via Leonardfactory's lf-policies-yields-preview API when that mod is installed. -->
					<Item>ui/dashboard/mad-preview-ids.js</Item>
					<Item>ui/dashboard/mad-dashboard.js</Item>
					<!-- Policy/Tradition card branding (Option B: lane colours + emblem badge). Exports
					     MAD_EMBLEM_SVG, which the dock decorator imports for the "Emblem" icon option. -->
					<Item>ui/cards/mad-card-brand.js</Item>
					<Item>ui/dock/mad-dock-decorator.js</Item>
					<!-- Surveyor selected-unit portrait fix (2026-07-12): repoints the live 3D render at the
					     Migrant donor so the panel square isn't black (cosmetic, fail-safe decorator). -->
					<Item>ui/surveyor/mad-surveyor-portrait.js</Item>
					<!-- Ascendancy boost glow (2026-07-13): Civ6-style blue glow on nodes whose boost deed is
					     earned. Reads the per-node MA_BOOST_* player property set by the boost markers. -->
					<Item>ui/boostui/mad-boost-glow.js</Item>
					<!-- Tree tooltip UX (2026-07-26, GitHub #27; litmus-proven): the cost pill becomes the
					     progress bar on ALL trees (base + Ascendancy + choosers), and the Ascendancy boost
					     deed consolidates into ONE box above the cost (card badge says WHETHER, box says
					     WHAT; completed nodes show nothing boost-related). Portal-observer patch on the
					     ui-next tooltip; pure additive UI. -->
					<Item>ui/treeux/mad-tree-tooltip.js</Item>
					<!-- MA-native card price tags (2026-07-22, task #27): live computed value chips on MA
					     cards in the policy chooser. Formula manifest is generated (gen-ascendancy.ps1);
					     the decorator computes from game state — works with or without the companion
					     preview mod, and hides the companion's floor-copy-only (zero-reading) box on MA
					     cards. Load order: formulas before the decorator. -->
					<Item>ui/cards/mad-card-formulas.js</Item>
					<Item>ui/cards/mad-card-chips.js</Item>
				</UIScripts>
				<UpdateText>
					<Item>text/en_us/DashboardText.xml</Item>
				</UpdateText>
			</Actions>
		</ActionGroup>
	</ActionGroups>
	<LocalizedText>
		<File>text/en_us/DashboardText.xml</File>
	</LocalizedText>
</Mod>
